<v Derrick Ingram>我心想，哦，该死，他们真的要走了。所以我没开门。

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:50.560
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>With no warning, no obvious reason, and almost as quickly as they'd shown up, the cops had packed up their stuff and bounced, just like that, after more than six hours. The moment the police left, John took off towards Derrick's building.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>毫无预兆，没有明显的理由，警察出现得有多快，撤退得就有多快，就这样，在持续了六个多小时后。警察离开的那一刻，约翰冲向德里克的楼房。

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:59.230
<v John Acosta>That was a good moment, man. I'd seen him, he'd seen me. He'd seen that it was somebody that loves him that was there. It wasn't some fucking cop ready to take him out and shit.
<v John Acosta>那是一个美好的时刻，伙计。我看到了他，他也看到了我。他看到了这里有爱他的人。不是什么他妈的警察准备带走他之类。

00:31:59.230 --> 00:31:59.950
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Did you just hug him?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>你刚才抱他了吗？

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:03.280
<v John Acosta>Hell yeah, man. I hugged the shit out of that man. I'm proud of that man, yo!
<v John Acosta>当然抱了，伙计。我狠狠地抱了那个男人。我为他感到自豪，兄弟！

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:08.770
<v Crowd>The people united will never be defeated. The people united--
<v Crowd>团结的人民永不被击败。团结的人民——

00:32:08.770 --> 00:32:34.540
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>The next day, Derrick went with his lawyer to turn himself in at the precinct. They figured he'd be safer that way so something like this wouldn't happen again. And that's when he found out the charges against him-- assault of a police officer for allegedly shouting into a cop's ear with a megaphone at a protest. They also charged him with obstructing governmental administration, which basically means getting in the way of a cop doing their job.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>第二天，德里克带着律师去警察局自首。他们觉得这样更安全，以免类似的事情再次发生。就在那时，他知道了针对他的指控——因涉嫌在抗议中用扩音器对着警察耳朵大喊，被控袭击警察。他们还指控他妨碍公务，这基本上意味着阻碍警察履行职责。

00:32:34.540 --> 00:33:06.250
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>I tried to get more information from the NYPD about why all this happened, but they wouldn't tell me anything other than a generic statement, saying a police officer had tried to stop Derrick from entering a, quote, "frozen zone" at a protest, and, quote, "a struggle ensued." I'm not sure that explains deploying the full force of one of the most militarized police departments in the country for one man for six hours.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我试图从纽约警局获得更多关于这件事为何发生的信息，但他们除了一个通用的声明外什么都不肯告诉，声明称一名警察试图阻止德里克进入抗议活动的所谓“冻结区”，随后“发生了冲突”。我不确定这能解释为何为一个男人部署了该国最军事化的警察部队之一，并持续了六个小时。

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:26.320
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>I couldn't think of any justification other than they wanted to intimidate Derrick and the growing movement against police violence. Derrick was shook. But he told me after the whole thing, he also felt pretty victorious. He had been able to capture the police abusing their power to intimidate an unarmed Black person.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我想不出任何理由，除了他们想恐吓德里克和日益壮大的反警察暴力运动。德里克被吓坏了。但他事后告诉我，他也感到相当胜利。他能够捕捉到警察滥用职权恐吓手无寸铁的黑人。

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:38.090
<v Derrick Ingram>Literally, that's what this whole movement is about. And that's what they did to me, publicly, in Hell's Kitchen in Manhattan. It made them look savage.
<v Derrick Ingram>从字面上看，这就是整场运动的意义所在。这就是他们在曼哈顿西地狱区公开对我做的事。这让他们看起来像个野蛮人。

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:56.520
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Derrick did have a second target on his back. He was Black, and now he was a prominent enough activist for the NYPD to come after him. But the Warriors' tactics, the thing they'd been doing all summer, made him safer. They won.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>德里克的背上确实贴了第二个靶子。他是黑人，现在他是一个足够著名的活动人士，以至于纽约警局对他下手了。但“战士”们的策略，他们整个夏天一直在做的事，让他更安全了。他们赢了。

00:33:56.520 --> 00:34:09.870
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>A little while later, I was talking to John on the phone about that day at Derrick's. He mentioned that he got a message from an anonymous account on Instagram. He hadn't noticed it at the time when he was focused on the cops. But later on--
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>过了一会儿，我在电话里跟约翰聊起德里克公寓那天的事。他提到他收到了 Instagram 上一个匿名账号的消息。他在关注警察时没注意到。但后来——

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:24.060
<v John Acosta>And as I'm looking, I'm like, what the hell is this? I open it, and it says, "You are next." I was like, what the fuck? Like, this is a threat.
<v John Acosta>我看着消息，心想，这他妈是什么？我打开它，上面写着，“你是下一个”。心想，这他妈是什么？这是威胁。

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:38.580
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>John laughed it off. He sent the guy an emoji of a middle finger and a message-- "You'll have to catch me first."
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>约翰把它当个笑话处理了。他发给那人一个中指表情符号和一条消息——“你得先抓到我再说”。

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:48.700
<v Emanuele Berry>Coming up after the break, revenge. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Emanuele Berry>中场休息后，复仇。更多内容，稍后从芝加哥公共广播电台继续节目。

00:34:48.700 --> 00:35:12.950
<v Emanuele Berry>It's This American Life. I'm Emanuele Berry, sitting in for Ira today. Today on our show, Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr. is telling the story of three activists in the group Warriors in the Garden, who, like Saidu, are trying to make sense of the question, what do you do when police violence finds you unwittingly? When we left before the break, the Warriors' activism had started to draw the attention of police. Here's Saidu again.
<v Emanuele Berry>这里是《美国生活》。我是埃马纽埃尔·贝里，今天代伊拉主持。在今天的节目中，小赛杜·特詹-托马斯讲述了“花园中的战士”团体中三位活动人士的故事，他们和赛杜一样，试图理清这个问题：当警察暴力莫名其妙地找上你时，你该怎么办？休息前我们说到，“战士”们的活动开始引起警方的注意。下面继续由赛杜讲述。

00:35:12.950 --> 00:35:32.030
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>By the end of last summer, almost all the Warriors said they'd gotten death threats on social media, all except John. John got his first one that day outside Derrick's apartment, when he was watching the police siege. He got in a message from an anonymous account on Instagram, saying, "You are next."
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>去年夏天快结束时，几乎所有的“战士”都说他们在社交媒体上收到过死亡威胁，除了约翰。约翰第一次收到威胁就是在那天德里克公寓外，他看着警察围困的时候。他收到一条 Instagram 匿名账号发来的消息，说“你是下一个”。

00:35:32.030 --> 00:36:01.860
<v John Acosta>I didn't really pay any mind to it like that because-- I asked people, because this is my first threat. I asked people. I was like, yo, I just got threatened. This is my first threat. What should I do? Oh, ignore it. I have so many. And I said, all right, I'm not going to be worried about it, you know? But I'm going to be wary. I'm going to be looking around, making sure everything's OK. And then, boom. Then I got caught with my pants down.
<v John Acosta>我并没有太放在心上，因为——我问过别人，因为这是我第一次收到威胁。我问别人，嘿，我刚受到威胁。这是第一次。我该怎么办？哦，忽略它。我有过很多次。我说，好吧，我不会担心，你知道的。但我会保持警惕。我会四处看看，确保一切安好。然后，砰。我这就被打了个措手不及。

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:10.710
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Within two weeks of the siege at Derrick's, John got on Instagram Live. He was holding up bloody wrists to the camera.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>在德里克公寓围困事件的两周内，约翰开了 Instagram 直播。他对着镜头举起鲜血淋漓的手腕。

00:36:10.710 --> 00:36:26.550
<v John Acosta>Hey, what's up, everyone? I just got hit by a car. I'm 100% sure they just-- they tried to kill me. They hit me and they booked it out of there.
<v John Acosta>嘿，大家好。我刚被车撞了。我百分之百肯定他们试图杀了我。他们撞了我，然后就逃之夭夭了。

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:38.140
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>He pans the camera to show a couple of nasty scrapes on both his legs. He was riding his bike when he got hit. I can tell John is shaken up, as he tells the people watching what just happened.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>他移动镜头展示了双腿上几处严重的擦伤。他骑自行车时被撞的。我能感觉到约翰心有余悸，他讲述着刚才发生的事。

00:36:38.140 --> 00:37:00.030
<v John Acosta>I'm moving out of his way. I'm moving more to my lane, and he just jumps the line and hits me, looks back, and then bounces. The man sees me. It's like he seen me. Clear-- I'm wearing fucking red. What the fuck? It's mad lit out here.
<v John Acosta>我从他旁边移开。我移向自己的车道，他突然变道撞向我，回头看了一眼，然后跑了。那家伙看见我了。就像他确实看见我了。清楚得很——我穿着红衣服。他妈的怎么回事？外面很亮啊。

00:37:00.030 --> 00:37:08.940
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>John says he looked up at the car as it drove away. A Chevy Impala. Chevy Impalas are the kind of cars the cops are known to drive when they're undercover.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>约翰说他抬头看了一眼汽车离去。一辆雪佛兰 Impala。雪佛兰 Impala 是警察便衣巡逻时常用的车型。

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:40.290
<v John Acosta>Shit, man. I don't even know what to really think right now. I just wanted to let y'all know because this is some crazy shit right now. I can't even call the fucking police because they're not going to do shit. Fuck 12. So I don't know if there's a way that I can prove that it was them. I'm looking for some cameras, but anybody that's out here in these streets, man, just be careful.
<v John Acosta>该死，伙计。我现在甚至不知道该想什么。我只是想让你们知道，因为这太疯狂了。我甚至不能报警，因为他们根本不会做任何事。去他的警察。所以不知道有没有办法证明是他们干的。我在找监控摄像头，但任何在街上的人，伙计，一定要小心。

00:37:40.290 --> 00:38:15.710
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>With protesting, Chi, Derrick, and John have all faced off with police. But John has spent years dealing with cops threatening his life. He grew up in the Bronx, where cops are notorious for harassing the Black and Brown people who live there. When he was a kid, John says cops showed up to his house and pulled a gun out on him and his mom in their home. They were looking for somebody else. Later, in high school, he and his friends were celebrating graduation at one of their houses. He says the cops showed up then, too, kicked down the door, and pointed guns at them.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>通过抗议，Chi、德里克和约翰都曾与警察对峙。但约翰多年来一直应对警察对其生命的威胁。他在布朗克斯长大，那里警察骚扰黑人和棕色人种臭名昭著。约翰说，他小时候警察曾到他家，在家里用枪指着他和妈妈。他们当时在找别人。后来，高中毕业时，他和朋友们在其中一家庆祝。他说警察也去了那里，踹开门，拿枪指着他们。

00:38:15.710 --> 00:38:33.660
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>John joined the Warriors because they were the community he'd been looking for, the people who would help them fight back against this enemy that loomed large over his life long before last summer. But when he got hit by the Impala, something started to switch for John. I called him up a few days later.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>约翰加入“战士”是因为他们是他一直在寻找的社区，人们会帮他们反击这个早在去年夏天之前就笼罩在他生命中的敌人。但当他被 Impala 撞倒后，约翰的某些东西开始转变。几天后我给他打了电话。

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:46.340
<v John Acosta>This shit changes everything. Cops did this shit to me. That's what I believe, because it was a fucking Impala. These motherfuckers tried to take me away from my family, man. All I want now, I want revenge.
<v John Acosta>这该死的一切改变了一切。是警察对我干的。我相信是这样，因为那是一辆 Impala。这些混蛋试图把我和家人分开。我现在想要的只有复仇。

00:38:46.340 --> 00:39:11.070
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>This was the first time I had heard any Warrior talk about vengeance. They talked about not backing down, about marching until they could secure some real wins for the movement in New York City, but never in the context of revenge. The Warriors were deeply committed to nonviolence, and John was committed to the Warriors, so he'd agreed to that, too. But after the hit and run, he was questioning everything.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>这是我第一次听到有“战士”谈论报复。他们谈论永不退缩，谈论游行直到在纽约市为运动赢得真正的胜利，但从未在复仇的语境下讨论。他们深信非暴力，“战士”们也都如此，所以约翰也同意。但在那次肇事逃逸后，他开始质疑一切。

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:55.980
<v John Acosta>You think I give a fuck about some peaceful protest now? Nah, man. I didn't do shit, bro. Every single time one of these motherfuckers is in my face, doing some bullshit, I don't knock them out. I don't kick them in the dick. I don't kick them in the knee. I don't kick them in the face. I don't do any-- I just go, aight, that's cool. And I take the high road. This was an attempt on my life, man. I'm not going around sleeping anymore. Like, that shit woke me the fuck up. It woke me the fuck up.
<v John Acosta>你觉得我现在还他妈在乎什么和平抗议吗？不，伙计。我什么都没做，兄弟。每次这些混蛋面对我、做些破事时，我都没有打倒他们。我没有踢他们裆部。我没有踢他们膝盖。我没有踢他们脸。我什么都没做——我只是说，好吧，没关系。然后我采取了高姿态。这是一次针对我生命的企图，伙计。我再也睡不着了。这事儿彻底把我惊醒了。彻彻底底。

00:39:55.980 --> 00:40:22.740
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>I have no way of knowing if the car that hit John was an undercover cop. The NYPD did not respond to our questions about this incident. But John didn't want to take any more chances. He told me he tried to get security camera footage from a nearby business to try and prove that it was a cop who hit him. But he says they wouldn't give it to him. So now he just doesn't leave the house without strapping a GoPro camera to his chest so he can review his own footage every night when he comes home.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我无法确定撞约翰的车是否是便衣警察。纽约警局没有回应我们就此事件提出的问题。但约翰不想再冒任何险。他告诉我，他试图获取附近商家的安全监控录像以证明是警察撞了他。但他说他们不给。所以现在，他如果不胸前绑上 GoPro 摄像机绝不出门，这样他每晚回家都能查看自己的录像。

00:40:22.740 --> 00:41:03.510
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>He admitted he was paranoid, but I understood where he was coming from. He just watched his friend be trapped in his apartment for six hours by the NYPD. If Derrick wasn't on Instagram Live the whole time, who would have believed the shit they pulled? John didn't have evidence. He just had his word and plenty of examples to point to of police forces surveilling, harassing, or killing Black leaders. MLK, Fred Hampton, Nina Simone, Malcolm X-- the list goes on. John told me he started pulling away from the Warriors. The protests with all the singing and dancing just didn't seem right. It felt too much like a parade, instead of a protest.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>他承认自己有被害妄想，但我理解他的感受。他刚看着朋友被纽约警局困在公寓里六个小时。如果德里克没全程开启 Instagram 直播，谁会相信他们搞的那一套？约翰没有证据。他只有自己的一面之词，以及警察监视、骚扰或杀害黑人领袖的众多例子。马丁·路德·金、弗雷德·汉普顿、尼娜·西蒙、马尔科姆·X——名单不胜枚举。约翰告诉我，他开始从“战士”中撤出。那些又唱又跳的抗议看起来一点都不对劲。看起来更像是游行，而不是抗议。

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:35.670
<v John Acosta>I love it when they're happy. I do, man. It makes me happy, too, man. But we just have no time for that. And when I seen them, I just feel like they're losing sight of what's going on. I've been thinking about that solid, like solid. All this bull, and we out here giving out Black joy. I was fine with it for a little bit. Then I started to realize, yo, what the fuck is going on? I looked at myself. I'm like, where the fuck is the rage, bro? I'm not feeling this no more.
<v John Acosta>当他们开心时我很高兴。真的，伙计。那也让我开心。但我们没时间搞这个。看到他们时，我觉得他们忽视了正在发生的事情。我一直在深刻思考这一点。所有这些废话，我们却在这里分发“黑人的快乐”。我有一阵子还能接受。然后我开始意识到，嘿，这他妈是怎么回事？我审视自己。我在想，愤怒去哪儿了，兄弟？我再也感觉不到那个了。

00:41:35.670 --> 00:41:56.340
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>The Warrior stance is that Black joy, that celebrating in Black life in spite of everything that's trying to keep you down, is a radical form of protest. You take up space in the streets. You dance and chant unapologetically. But you don't engage with the police. You keep it moving. But John didn't want to hear that anymore.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>“战士”的立场是，“黑人的快乐”，即在试图压垮你的一切面前依然庆祝黑人的生活，是一种激进的抗议方式。你在街道上占据空间。你毫不妥协地跳舞和高喊。但你不要与警察产生纠缠。继续前行就好。但约翰不想再听这些了。

00:41:56.340 --> 00:42:39.400
<v John Acosta>Let me ask a fucking question. When Breonna Taylor was slain in her bedroom, was she giving out Black joy? Was she listening to music? Was she bopping her fucking head? No, no music, it was just rings-- gunshots. Why the fuck are we giving them our Black joy? They don't deserve our Black joy. They deserve the Black rage we have, every ounce of it. This is war.
<v John Acosta>让我问个他妈的问题。当布伦娜·泰勒在卧室里被杀时，她在分享“黑人的快乐”吗？她在听音乐吗？她在点头吗？不，没有音乐，只有枪声。我们为什么要给他们分享我们的“黑人的快乐”？他们不配得到我们的快乐。他们应得的是我们心中的“黑人的愤怒”，每一分每一毫的愤怒。这是战争。

00:42:39.400 --> 00:43:08.590
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>There was a truth to John's anger that was hard to look away from. I recognized his rage. When Marcus, my friend, was killed by the cops, I didn't realize how angry I was until I walked into a chicken spot on my block, and I saw a customer threatening to fight my favorite cashier. I didn't even know the cashier that well, but I wanted to beat the brakes off this customer for no good reason, other than he was a threat to somebody I knew, somebody I liked.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>约翰的愤怒中有一种让人无法移开视线的真相。我认出了这种愤怒。当我的朋友马库斯被警察杀害时，我直到走进街区的一家炸鸡店看到顾客威胁要打我最喜欢的收银员时，才意识到自己有多愤怒。我甚至不太认识那个收银员，但我却想把那个顾客往死里揍，没有别的理由，只是因为他是某种我知道、我很喜欢的人的威胁。

00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:36.290
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>But nothing happened. So I just wandered aimlessly around my block and considered walking into traffic. But I never did. I just swallowed my anger and pushed it somewhere I could never access it again. When I met John, he seemed to be at a similar crossroads-- swallow his anger or do something with it. And by now, I knew from listening to him that he's not the type to walk away from a fight.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>但什么也没发生。于是我只是漫无目的地在街区徘徊，考虑着走进车流。但我从未那么做。我只是吞下愤怒，把它推到我永远无法触及的地方。当我见到约翰时，他似乎也站在类似的十字路口——吞下愤怒，还是用它做点什么。到目前为止，从他的话语中我知道，他不是那种会退缩的人。

00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:48.240
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>The way you move and the way you use your anger, I don't know. It feels like maybe the anger helps somehow. There's something good about it. There's something that I could actually learn from it.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>你的行事方式和使用愤怒的方式，我不知道。感觉也许这种愤怒在某种程度上是有帮助的。它有某种好处。有些东西我真的可以从中学习。

00:43:48.240 --> 00:44:31.390
<v John Acosta>So what the anger does, it takes that person that is afraid and tells it, shut the hell up. Let's go. I think you not allowing yourself to continue to fight got you mad. And I think who you were mad at was not on anybody else but yourself, because you knew what you were doing was wrong. So you weren't mad-- you were upset with the police at what they did, hell yeah. But the person that you were really mad at was yourself because you were walking away from what you were believing in. You gave up on you.
<v John Acosta>愤怒的作用是，它能抓住那个恐惧的人并告诉他，闭嘴。我们走吧。我觉得你没允许自己继续战斗才让你愤怒。我觉得你愤怒的不是别人，而是你自己，因为你知道你所做的是错的。所以你并不生气——你对警察的行为感到失望，当然是的。但你真正生气的人是你自己，因为你背离了你的信仰。你放弃了你自己。

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:52.660
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>One day, at the end of one of our conversations in September, John invited me to a protest. Maybe he was trying to help me see something, or maybe he wanted to prove to me that he wasn't all talk. But he told me to meet him at a park in the Bronx the next day.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>九月的一次谈话结束时，约翰邀请我去参加一场抗议。也许他是想帮我看到些什么，或者他想向我证明他不是光说不练。他让我第二天去布朗克斯的一个公园找他。

00:44:52.660 --> 00:45:07.660
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>It's about 4:00 PM when I pull up. There's a small crowd gathered at the center of the park, no more than 100 people. And the first thing you hear when you're approaching the crowd is something like, if you're not comfortable being arrested, this is not the place for you.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>下午4点左右我到了。公园中心聚集了一小群人，不超过100个。当你接近人群时，你听到的第一件事就是，如果你不想被捕，这里不适合你。

00:45:07.660 --> 00:45:12.040
<v Woman>I know some of us are and might be. We don't like those fascists, but we know--
<v Woman>我知道我们中有些人是或者可能是。我们不喜欢那些法西斯，但我们知道——

00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:41.110
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>It's not a Warriors protest. This one was organized by a couple other groups John's been spending more time with since his hit-and-run. They're protesters who are a little more willing to antagonize the cops. I find out from somebody what I already suspected. The goal of this protest is to march onto the George Washington Bridge and shut it down. There's a few Warriors who have shown up. But I don't see Derrick. He told me later that this was not his kind of protest.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>这不是“战士”的抗议。这场是由约翰在肇事逃逸后花更多时间在一起的其他几个团体组织的。他们是更愿意对抗警察的抗议者。我从某人那里证实了我预感到的事。这场抗议的目标是游行到乔治·华盛顿大桥并将其封锁。有几个“战士”出现了。但我没看到德里克。他后来告诉我这不是他那种抗议。

00:45:41.110 --> 00:45:55.210
<v Derrick Ingram>I said, John, I don't think the Warriors should participate, and I cannot participate. I think you like the idea of taking a bridge because it sounds cool. I didn't hear any demands. I didn't hear an endgame. I didn't know what a resolution was.
<v Derrick Ingram>我说，约翰，我不觉得“战士”们应该参加，我也不能参加。我觉得你喜欢夺桥这个主意，因为它听起来很酷。我没听到什么诉求。我没听到什么终局。我不知道解决方案是什么。

00:45:55.210 --> 00:46:18.760
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>There may not have been a specific set of demands, but there was an endgame. And it seems like the cops know about this plan to take the bridge, too. Because just a few feet away from where we're standing, patrol cars and vans are starting to pull up to follow the march, a long row of red and blue lights flashing down the street for blocks. I start looking around the crowd for John, but I spot Chi instead.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>可能没有一套具体的诉求，但有一个终局。看起来警察也知道这个占领大桥的计划。因为离我们站的地方仅几英尺远的地方，巡逻车和面包车开始靠近以跟随游行，长长一排红蓝警灯闪烁着横跨几个街区。我开始在人群中寻找约翰，却发现了Chi。

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:21.970
<v Chi Osse>You like my collared shirt today? Do I look like a politician today?
<v Chi Osse>你喜欢我今天的衬衫吗？我今天看起来像个政治家吗？

00:46:21.970 --> 00:46:24.760
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>You do look like a politician today.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>你今天看起来确实像个政治家。

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:26.080
<v Chi Osse>Nice.
<v Chi Osse>不错。

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:34.720
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>In his sweater/collared shirt combo, Chi tells me he's been asked to give a speech. He walks up onto a makeshift podium in front of the crowd.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>穿着毛衣配衬衫，Chi告诉我他被邀请发表演讲。他走上人群前方的一个简易讲台。

00:46:34.720 --> 00:46:43.390
<v Chi Osse>How are we going to change the course of this war, the course of this battle against criminal injustice in New York City, criminal injustice--
<v Chi Osse>我们将如何改变这场战争的进程，这场对抗纽约市刑事不公的战斗的进程，刑事不公——

00:46:43.390 --> 00:46:55.240
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Like John, Chi also believes that they're in a war. But he believes it's a war best fought through legislation, from inside the walls of city council, once he wins-- not on the George Washington Bridge.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>像约翰一样，Chi也相信他们正在战争中。但他相信这场战争最好通过立法来打，一旦他获胜，就从市议会的墙内打，而不是在乔治·华盛顿大桥上。

00:46:55.240 --> 00:46:59.770
<v Chi Osse>Our ancestors have fought so hard for the right to vote.
<v Chi Osse>我们的祖先为了投票权奋斗得太艰难了。

00:46:59.770 --> 00:47:03.470
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Once he finishes his speech, Chi gets ready to leave.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>演讲结束后，Chi准备离开。

00:47:03.470 --> 00:47:06.280
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>So you're about to leave?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>所以你要走了？

00:47:06.280 --> 00:47:07.120
<v Chi Osse>Mm-hmm.
<v Chi Osse>嗯。

00:47:07.120 --> 00:47:07.990
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Why don't you want to stay?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>为什么不想留下来？

00:47:07.990 --> 00:47:15.010
<v Chi Osse>Because I have other things to do today. I have other campaign matters to attend to today.
<v Chi Osse>因为我今天还有别的事要做。我今天还有竞选事项要处理。

00:47:15.010 --> 00:47:16.670
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>All right, man. Be safe.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>好吧，伙计。保重。

00:47:16.670 --> 00:47:48.190
<v Chi Osse>And tomorrow. Yeah. Yeah, you, too. The cops are already out here today. You can tell that the energy is a little different here in the Bronx in terms of just the police that have already arrived. My power is outside of those bars. I will not be arrested. I won't. There's some of-- OK, there's some people here-- I'm not snitching-- in bulletproof vests. It's not my energy. I can't be doing that.
<v Chi Osse>明天见。是啊，你也是。警察今天已经在这儿了。你能感觉到在布朗克斯，就警察已经到达的数量而言，气氛有点不同。我的力量在这些铁栏之外。我不会被捕的。我不会。这里有些人——我不是打小报告——穿着防弹背心。这不是我的能量。我不能搞那个。

00:47:48.190 --> 00:48:16.940
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>This did not calm my nerves, because Chi was right. What the hell was I doing out here? But before I can think about it too hard, I finally spot John. He's dressed in all black-- black pants, black long-sleeved shirt, black Kaepernick jersey, black ski mask, and a black bulletproof vest. Just as Chi's about to leave, John calls him over. They chat, they hug for a second, and afterwards, I walk over to John.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>这并没有让我的神经平复，因为Chi是对的。我在这里干什么鬼？但在我仔细思考之前，我终于看到了约翰。他全身黑衣——黑裤子、黑色长袖衬衫、黑色卡佩尼克球衣、黑色滑雪面罩和黑色防弹背心。就在Chi准备离开时，约翰把他叫了过去。他们交谈，抱了一下，随后，我走向约翰。

00:48:16.940 --> 00:48:17.950
<v John Acosta>I'm not surprised myself.
<v John Acosta>我没把自己感到惊讶。

00:48:17.950 --> 00:48:21.580
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>What's up, man? How are you doing?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>怎么样，伙计？你好吗？

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:23.470
<v John Acosta>Disappointed.
<v John Acosta>很失望。

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:23.980
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Why?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>为什么？

00:48:23.980 --> 00:48:40.440
<v John Acosta>I'm proud that everybody's out here that wanted to come out here because it just shows me who has heart. But then it just shows how many people are cappin', how many people are just running their mouth. I'm not an asshole. I love my squad. But where's Chi now?
<v John Acosta>我为每一个想来的人都来了感到自豪，因为这向我展示了谁有胆量。但这也显示了多少人在吹牛，多少人只是信口雌黄。我不是混蛋。我爱我的小队。但Chi现在在哪儿？

00:48:40.440 --> 00:48:43.470
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Not long after, the protest starts to move.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>没多久，抗议开始移动。

00:48:43.470 --> 00:48:50.160
<v Man>It's important that we keep it tight and that we keep it structured. All right? Who's with me?
<v Man>重要的是我们保持紧凑和有组织。好吗？谁跟我一起？

00:48:50.160 --> 00:48:51.880
<v Man>[CHEERING]
<v Man>[欢呼]

00:48:51.880 --> 00:48:58.130
<v Man>If you're with me, put your fist in the air! If you're with me, put your fist in the air.
<v Man>如果跟我一起，把拳头举向空中！如果跟我一起，把拳头举向空中。

00:48:58.130 --> 00:49:25.580
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>The George Washington Bridge is a major revenue source for the city. The protesters believe if they shut it down, they'll cost the city money, get a lot of news coverage, and a lot of attention for the movement, too. As we march from the park into the streets of the Bronx, John moved through the small crowd, checking in on people. After a while, we finally reached the George Washington Bridge. And when John sees it, his eyes seem to light up through the holes in a ski mask.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>乔治·华盛顿大桥是城市的主要收入来源。抗议者认为如果他们封锁它，就会让城市损失金钱，获得大量新闻报道，也能为运动带来关注。当我们从公园游行进入布朗克斯街道时，约翰在人群中穿梭，查看大家的情况。过了一会儿，我们终于到达了乔治·华盛顿大桥。当约翰看到它时，他的眼睛似乎在滑雪面罩的孔后闪闪发光。

00:49:25.580 --> 00:49:27.140
<v John Acosta>This is the bridge.
<v John Acosta>这就是大桥。

00:49:27.140 --> 00:49:29.260
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Oh, shit.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>哦，该死。

00:49:29.260 --> 00:49:34.640
<v John Acosta>Watch this magic. Watch. Watch this beautiful shit.
<v John Acosta>看这魔法。看。看这美妙的狗屁东西。

00:49:34.640 --> 00:49:46.670
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>I'd never seen the George Washington Bridge on foot, all 4,760 feet of it just towering right in front of us. I look over at John, and he's having a moment.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我从没徒步走过乔治·华盛顿大桥，全长4760英尺，就在我们面前耸立。我看向约翰，他正在享受这一刻。

00:49:46.670 --> 00:50:06.250
<v John Acosta>Oh, yes! Oh, yes! My heart is racing, and I feel so good! Yo, I feel like a-- I feel like an orgasm. I feel like I'm about to hoop-- ah-- all over the NYPD, baby!
<v John Acosta>哦，是的！哦，是的！我的心跳加速，感觉太棒了！伙计，我觉得像个——我觉得像是在——啊——在那群纽约警察身上高潮了，宝贝！

00:50:06.250 --> 00:50:10.670
<v Man>For too long, we're taking half steps. This is a full measure. Are you ready?
<v Man>太久以来，我们走的都是小步子。这是一个完整的举措。准备好了吗？

00:50:10.670 --> 00:50:11.600
<v Crowd>Yeah!
<v Crowd>准备好了！

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:14.320
<v Man>Then let's go.
<v Man>那就走吧。

00:50:14.320 --> 00:50:39.490
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>John rushes to the on-ramp with the other protesters. They grab giant construction cones and place them in front of traffic. And pretty soon, the whole upper deck of the bridge, eight lanes wide, from New York to New Jersey, there's no cars-- just this small group of protesters suspended above the Hudson River, standing shoulder to shoulder, arms linked, hyped about what they're accomplishing. I find John darting around the crowd.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>约翰和抗议者们冲向坡道。他们抓起巨大的施工路锥放在车流前。很快，大桥整个上层，八车道宽，从纽约到新泽西，没有车——只有这群挂在哈德逊河上方、肩并肩、手挽手的抗议者，为他们所完成的事情而兴奋不已。我发现约翰在人群中穿梭。

00:50:39.490 --> 00:50:41.420
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>How does it feel, bro?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>感觉如何，兄弟？

00:50:41.420 --> 00:50:58.560
<v John Acosta>We the few! We the few are many! That's how I feel. Look at the power we have! And none of our brothers and sisters came out to support us! None!
<v John Acosta>我们是少数！我们少数人就是多数！这就是我的感觉。看看我们拥有的力量！而且我们那些兄弟姐妹一个都没出来支持我们！一个都没有！

00:50:58.560 --> 00:51:23.550
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>John and the other activists keep the upper level of the bridge shut down for about an hour. The whole time we're up there, the police are nearby, watching, looking annoyed. They had the bridge, but what these protesters really wanted was to show how violent the cops can be. They want to prove the police will act with impunity, hopefully get it caught on camera, it goes viral, and they make their point.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>约翰和其他活动人士将大桥上层封锁了大约一小时。我们在那儿的整个时间，警察都在附近盯着，一脸厌恶。他们占领了大桥，但这些抗议者真正想要的是展示警察有多暴力。他们想证明警察会肆无忌惮地行动，希望这被摄像头拍下，流传开来，以此表达他们的观点。

00:51:23.550 --> 00:51:44.010
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>After 45 minutes, the cops finally move them off the bridge relatively peacefully. But unsatisfied, the group marches towards a nearby precinct. And the whole way there, more cops start to appear from the dark, dressed in riot gear right behind us. When the group gets to the precinct, there's more cops waiting out front, too.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>45分钟后，警察最终相对和平地将他们从大桥上驱离。但意犹未尽，该团体向附近的警察局游行。在那条路上，更多警察从黑暗中出现，穿着防暴装备跟在我们身后。当该团体到达警察局时，前面也有更多警察在等着。

00:51:44.010 --> 00:51:50.670
<v Man>This is where we stand. This is where we stand. We got it jammed up. This is where we stand.
<v Man>这就是我们的立场。这就是我们的立场。我们把这儿堵上了。这就是我们的立场。

00:51:50.670 --> 00:52:09.360
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>At that point, the cops have had enough. They gave a quick dispersal warning. Not long enough for people to actually respond to, but long enough to say they did it. I got the sense that they knew what this was, too. So they gave them what they came for. They started charging towards us and violently snatching people out of the crowd.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>那一刻，警察受够了。他们迅速发出了驱散警告。时间短到人们无法真正回应，但足够让他们说自己已经警告过。我感觉到他们也知道这是怎么回事。所以他们给了他们想要的结果。他们开始向我们冲锋，暴力地将人从人群中拽出来。

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:15.540
<v Cop>This is the New York City Police Department. You are unlawfully in the roadway and obstructing vehicular traffic.
<v Cop>这里是纽约警察局。你们在路面上非法滞留并阻碍车辆交通。

00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:40.380
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>We all started running away. And in the chaos of bodies bumping into each other, I tripped over somebody trying to escape. I quickly got back up and backed away from the crowd to look at what was happening from a safer distance. And some people were crying. Others were screaming in pain. And John was still darting around the crowd. It definitely felt like a war, and I think some people didn't realize that's what they were signing up for.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我们都开始逃跑。在肢体碰撞的混乱中，我被试图逃跑的人绊倒了。我很快站起来，退到离人群更安全的地方看发生了什么。有些人哭了。其他人痛苦地尖叫。而约翰还在人群中穿梭。这确实感觉像一场战争，我想有些人没意识到那是他们所签名的内容。

00:52:40.380 --> 00:53:07.800
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>I saw John walk up to a couple who were frozen with fear and crying in each other's arms, and he told them to go home, to get out of here. The night ended with at least six people arrested. One of them was a good friend of John's. I understood then why Derrick didn't show up for this and why Chi left. It all felt pointless. When I spoke to John later, I told him that.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我看到约翰走到一对冻结在恐惧中并抱头痛哭的恋人面前，告诉他们回家，离开这里。当晚结束时，至少有六人被捕。其中一个是约翰的好朋友。我当时理解了德里克为什么没来，为什么Chi离开了。这一切看起来毫无意义。后来我跟约翰谈起时，我说了。

00:53:07.800 --> 00:53:23.610
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>When we went out there, by the end of it, people were trampling over each other, dog. People were literally crying. I don't know. It just didn't seem like the anger was useful in that moment. It seemed like it was more destructive than anything.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>当我们出去时，最后人们在那儿相互踩踏，伙计。人们真的哭了。我不知道。只是觉得那时的愤怒并没有什么用。看起来比什么都更具破坏性。

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:25.210
<v John Acosta>You didn't see it then.
<v John Acosta>你那时没看见。

00:53:25.210 --> 00:53:26.070
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Say it again?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>再说一遍？

00:53:26.070 --> 00:53:28.290
<v John Acosta>You didn't see it then.
<v John Acosta>你那时没看见。

00:53:28.290 --> 00:53:35.640
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>John tells me the goal here was to expose the police and their brutality, that this is how they will make sure their message is heard.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>约翰告诉我，这里的目标是揭露警察及其暴行，这就是他们确保自己的信息被听到的方式。

00:53:35.640 --> 00:54:03.830
<v John Acosta>Isn't that the point, to prove how disgusting the system is? I am looking at the goal. The goal for me is abolishing the police, tearing down the whole system. So, in order to have everybody see why, because there's people that don't see why. Let me show you why. Keep exposing them. Keep exposing them. Keep exposing them until everybody sees.
<v John Acosta>难道目标不是证明这个制度有多恶心吗？我看着目标。对我的目标是废除警察，拆掉整个体制。所以，为了让所有人看到为什么，因为有些人看不到。让我告诉你为什么。继续揭露他们。继续揭露他们。继续揭露他们，直到所有人都能看到。

00:54:03.830 --> 00:54:10.740
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>I don't know. I just hope that there's a more safer way to do it.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我不知道。我只是希望有一种更安全的方式。

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:26.510
<v John Acosta>There's not. And if there was, we would have gone about it that way. But every time that we go safely about it, nothing happens. There is no safe way of doing this. I'm sorry.
<v John Acosta>没有。如果有，我们早就那样做了。但每一次我们以安全的方式行事时，什么都不会发生。没有安全的做法，伙计。对不起。

00:54:26.510 --> 00:55:06.290
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>I did see the thing John wanted me to see. I saw that the police were being unnecessarily violent. I'd experienced that at lots of protests. But I also saw this other thing. I saw John telling people to get to safety and trying to pull protesters out of the grasp of cops, but he couldn't save everyone. He couldn't be everywhere at once. If you are running headfirst into a war, you're going to lose people. But I guess we're losing people either way.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>我确实看到了约翰想让我看到的东西。我看到了警察正在无理取闹地暴力行事。我在许多抗议活动中都经历过。但我也看到了另一面。我看到约翰告诉人们注意安全，试图把抗议者从警察手中拉出来，但他无法拯救所有人。他不可能同时出现在任何地方。如果你一头扎进战争，你就会失去人。但我想我们无论如何都在失去人。

00:55:06.290 --> 00:55:34.200
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>The Warriors felt like John was crossing the line they'd set as a group at the beginning of all this. For a nonviolent group, John was flirting too much with chaos and vengeance. They told him they were worried about him and encouraged him to take better care of his mental health because they knew John suffered from depression, and he was off his meds. They had a series of disagreements about how to move forward with John. But eventually, they decided to just air it all out on a Zoom call. Here's Derrick.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>“战士”们觉得约翰越过了他们作为一个团体在这一切开始时划下的底线。作为一个非暴力团体，约翰在混沌和复仇的边缘试探得太多了。他们告诉他，他们为他担心，并鼓励他更好地照顾自己的心理健康，因为他们知道约翰患有抑郁症，而且他没吃药。他们就如何与约翰继续合作进行了一系列争论。但最终，他们决定在 Zoom 会议上摊开来说。这是德里克。

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:47.660
<v Derrick Ingram>He literally said, I want every single person to say their issue with me back to back. I won't interrupt you because I want to hear all of this. And I want to know how to respond.
<v Derrick Ingram>他直接说，我希望每个人都依次说出对我有什么意见。我不会打断你，因为我想听听所有这些。我想知道该如何回应。

00:55:47.660 --> 00:56:02.020
<v John Acosta>And immediately, it just turned into an attack on me. It didn't feel like they wanted to keep me. It felt like they were just trying to find a way to out me.
<v John Acosta>立刻，这变成了一场对我的攻击。感觉他们不是想留住我。感觉他们只是在找方法把我踢出去。

00:56:02.020 --> 00:56:06.600
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>What was the first thing they said?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>他们说的第一件事是什么？

00:56:06.600 --> 00:56:17.570
<v John Acosta>Kiara started. It was she doesn't trust me because of-- I'm a liability or something like that. I'm a risk or some shit.
<v John Acosta>Kiara 先说的。她说她不信任我，因为——我是个负担什么的。我是个风险之类。

00:56:17.570 --> 00:56:41.140
<v Kiara>I told John, I said I personally would not like you in the group anymore because of your actions. Personally, we all love John personally. But when you see kind of the shift in character and a shift in beliefs, we had to come to an agreement. Like, OK, what's best for the group?
<v Kiara>我告诉约翰，我说我个人因为你的行为不希望你再留在组里。我们个人都爱约翰。但当你看到性格和信仰的转变时，我们不得不达成协议。比如，好吧，什么对团体最好？

00:56:41.140 --> 00:56:57.610
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>The Warriors told John that if he was going to stay in the group, he'd need to commit to three things. First, that he'd make his mental health a priority. Second, he'd need to pledge his loyalty to the group. And third-- maybe the biggest point-- that he'd need to commit to being non-violent.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>“战士”们告诉约翰，如果他要留在组里，需要承诺三件事。第一，他要优先考虑心理健康。第二，他要宣誓忠于团体。第三——也许是最重要的一点——他要承诺非暴力。

00:56:57.610 --> 00:57:05.500
<v Derrick Ingram>But he didn't want to really address our three demands. And he couldn't. He couldn't commit to them.
<v Derrick Ingram>但他真的不想回应我们的三项要求。他做不到。他不能承诺那些。

00:57:05.500 --> 00:57:19.910
<v John Acosta>And I just couldn't focus. I was mad, and I was angry, and it wasn't supposed to go that way. It was supposed to be something really, really calm. Really, everybody talk respectfully. But nobody was doing that.
<v John Acosta>我当时就无法集中注意力了。我疯了，我很愤怒，这不该是这样的。这本该是真的很平静。确实，大家尊重地谈话。但没人那么做。

00:57:19.910 --> 00:57:32.280
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Finally, the Warriors decided to call a vote on whether John should be allowed to stay in the group or not. They voted no. Later in his apartment, John is still replaying how things went down.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>最后，“战士”们决定就约翰是否应被允许留在组里进行投票。他们投了反对票。后来在他的公寓里，约翰还在回味事情是怎么发生的。

00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:46.860
<v John Acosta>I-- this is fucking-- like, what's that shit called? Survival island or some shit like that? I got voted out. Like, what the fuck? [LAUGHS] That shit was unreal, son. It was unreal for me.
<v John Acosta>我——这他妈是——这叫什么来着？生存岛还是什么破玩意？我被投票踢出局了。这他妈是什么鬼？[笑] 这太不真实了，孩子。对我来说太不真实了。

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:57.300
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>He cycles through a bunch of emotions, laughing, shouting about it. On his bedroom wall, I see this black and white photo of the Warriors together from the beginning.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>他反复经历着各种情绪，大笑，大喊大叫。在他的卧室墙上，我看到那张“战士”们最初在一起的黑白照片。

00:57:57.300 --> 00:58:56.100
<v John Acosta>It was a family. I learned that it was like my family. I spoke to Derrick. Yo, Derrick, I love you, man. Don't get it twisted. I didn't like how the shit went down, but I love you. It just hurts. It hurts. They betrayed me, bro. [EXHALES] It's been hard, man. Yo, those Warriors, man, they betrayed me, man. They betrayed me, son. They left me out there in the cold to rot, bro. Did me dirt.
<v John Acosta>那是家人。我知道这就像我的家人。我跟德里克谈过。嘿，德里克，我爱你，伙计。别误会。我不喜欢事情的发展方式，但我爱你。只是很痛。很痛。他们背叛了我，兄弟。[呼气] 一直很艰难，伙计。那些“战士”，伙计，他们背叛了我，伙计。他们背叛了我，孩子。他们把我留在冷风中腐烂，兄弟。真缺德。

00:58:56.100 --> 00:59:36.340
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Ever since my friend Marcus was killed, I've been sitting with this question-- what am I supposed to do with this weight? With the Warriors, I think I had this expectation that when police violence lands in your lap, there's only one right way to deal with it. I thought with John, Chi, and Derrick, I might finally figure out what that one way was. But the three of them have each landed in three very different places. John is still running headfirst towards cops at protests and getting arrested, now with a different group of people. He's not playing it safe, and he's OK with that.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>自从我的朋友马库斯被杀后，我一直被这个问题困扰——我该怎么处理这块重量？对于“战士”，我想我曾有过这种期望，即当警察暴力降临时，只有一种正确方式来处理它。我想有了约翰、Chi和德里克，我可能终于能弄清楚那唯一的方式是什么了。但他们三个人每个人都走到了三个非常不同的终点。约翰依然在抗议中迎着警察冲锋并被捕，现在和一群不同的人在一起。他没想过安全行事，但他对此没意见。

00:59:36.340 --> 00:59:46.000
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Chi is nine months into his political campaign. I've gone out canvassing with him. Just the other day, I watched him basically obliterate his opponents at a debate.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Chi的政治竞选已经九个月了。我曾跟他出去挨家挨户拉票。就在几天前，我看着他在一场辩论中摧毁了他的对手。

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:50.480
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>Do you feel like you're becoming a better politician?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>你觉得你正在成为一个更好的政治家吗？

00:59:50.480 --> 01:00:02.380
<v Chi Osse>I don't like to consider myself a politician. I would say I'm more of a public advocate, and I'm running to be a public advocate. I'm a candidate for public advocacy.
<v Chi Osse>我不喜欢认为自己是政治家。我会说我更像是一个公共倡导者，我竞选是为了成为公共倡导者。我是公共倡导的候选人。

01:00:02.380 --> 01:00:06.160
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>So the answer is yes, you've become a better politician.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>所以答案是肯定的，你已经成为了一个更好的政治家。

01:00:06.160 --> 01:00:07.860
<v Chi Osse>Does it sound like I have?
<v Chi Osse>听起来像吗？

01:00:07.860 --> 01:00:16.540
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>You just sidestepped my question and put your own rhetoric out there in the most seamless way. So I think--
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>你刚才避开了我的问题，并以最无缝的方式表达了你自己的那一套。所以我认为——

01:00:16.540 --> 01:00:22.690
<v Chi Osse>I've become a better public advocate. That's the right answer.
<v Chi Osse>我成为了一个更好的公共倡导者。那是正确的答案。

01:00:22.690 --> 01:00:24.500
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>OK.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr.>好的。

01:00:24.500 --> 01:00:49.800
<v Emanuele Berry>Derrick is still going to protests all the time with the Warriors and Chi, but he's grown to be a high-profile activist now with a huge platform. He's now working with places like Amnesty International and Reebok to bring attention to police overreach. He's found his life's work. All three of them have figured out what I still haven't.
<v Emanuele Berry>德里克依然经常和“战士”们以及Chi一起去抗议，但他现在已经成长为一名拥有庞大平台的高调活动人士。他现在与国际特赦组织和锐步等机构合作，以引起对警察越权的关注。他找到了毕生的事业。他们三个人都弄清楚了我还没弄明白的事。

01:00:49.800 --> 01:01:23.730
<v Emanuele Berry>On Martin Luther King Day, there was a protest at Barclays Center in Brooklyn. And everyone was there-- Chi, John, and Derrick. By now, tensions had cooled, and they were all smiling and talking to each other, just catching up. At some point, dancing broke out, as it always does. People formed a circle, and Chi got in the middle for a second and hit a few moves. I looked off to the side, and Derrick was joining in. When I turned around, I couldn't believe it. There was John, getting low, dancing along with them.
<v Emanuele Berry>马丁·路德·金纪念日那天，布鲁克林巴克莱中心有一场抗议。每个人都在那里——Chi、约翰和德里克。到那时，紧张局势已经缓解，他们都在微笑并交谈，只是在叙旧。某个时刻，舞蹈开始了，一如往常。人们围成一个圈，Chi走到中间跳了几下。我看向旁边，德里克也加入了。当我转过身时，我简直不敢相信。约翰也在那里，压低身体，和他们一起跳着舞。

01:01:23.730 --> 01:02:05.370
<v Emanuele Berry>I'm always happy to see them together and enjoying themselves like this. I'm also haunted by this other thought. It's this. Think of all the other things these talented, creative people could have been spending their enormous energy on. How far could Chi have taken his love for fashion if he'd never seen that George Floyd video? What could John be doing with all the time he's been spending going to neurological appointments for injuries from being beaten up by cops? What if Derrick's apartment could have just stayed a party pad and didn't have to be the site of a police siege? Who could these three men have been if their paths weren't defined by these tragic events?
<v Emanuele Berry>我总是很高兴看到他们在一起并像这样享受时光。我也被另一个想法所困扰。是这个。想想这些才华横溢、富有创造力的人本可以把巨大的精力花在所有其他事情上。如果Chi从未看到那个乔治·弗洛伊德的视频，他对自己时尚的热爱能走多远？约翰把所有去神经科预约治疗被警察殴打造成的伤害的时间用在别处会怎样？如果德里克的公寓只是一间派对公寓，而不是警察围困的现场会怎样？如果这三个人的道路没有被这些悲惨事件所定义，他们本来会成为谁？

01:02:05.370 --> 01:02:43.800
<v Emanuele Berry>That day, they're dancing. And for a moment, they're just people. Chi is just a 22-year-old dude with his friends. John isn't angry, and Derrick gets to just be there for the both of them. I think I've been paralyzed by this question of how to respond to police violence. Because for me, every answer feels like another loss. Toward the end of the night, we march to City Hall. Then, the police started violently arresting people. That ended things-- the way they always end.
<v Emanuele Berry>那天，他们跳着舞。在那一刻，他们只是人。Chi只是一个和朋友在一起的22岁小伙子。约翰不生气，而德里克得以就在他们身边。我想我被如何回应警察暴力这个问题给困住了。因为对我来说，每一个答案都感觉是另一次损失。夜幕快降临时，我们游行到市政厅。然后，警察开始暴力逮捕人。那结束了一切——就像他们总是结束的方式那样。

01:02:43.800 --> 01:03:04.160
<v Emanuele Berry>[SHOUTING]
<v Emanuele Berry>[喊叫声]

01:03:04.160 --> 01:03:12.080
<v Emanuele Berry>Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr. You can find Saidu's show Resistance on Spotify or wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Emanuele Berry>小赛杜·特詹-托马斯。你可以在 Spotify 或任何你获取播客的地方找到赛杜的节目《抵抗》。

